If there are genuinely unresolved parenting issues, I wouldn't leave them to disappear just because messaging is triggering. I'd separate routine communication from issues that need a decision.

A workable structure could be:

Routine logistics: brief messages only.
Something needing discussion: put it on a shared list in the parenting app.
Discussion: use a scheduled call/video call, with one issue at a time.
Afterward: record the agreed outcome in the parenting app so neither of you has to rely on memory.
If you can't agree: leave it marked as unresolved and use whatever agreed third-party/mediation process you have, rather than continuing an escalating message exchange.
Only address issues that actually need resolving. Not every disagreement needs a conversation or a mutually agreed interpretation.

If you want to add this to the Facebook message, I'd keep it very simple:

Writing

For anything that genuinely needs to be discussed or resolved, we can put it in the parenting app and then deal with it by a scheduled call or video call, one issue at a time. We can then put any agreement or outcome back in the app so it’s clear for both of us.

If something doesn’t actually require a decision, I think it’s better to leave it rather than have extended discussions about it.
